  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Machine Learning in Python",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What tools can help me build a machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, students step into the role of machine learning engineers and meet scikit-learn, the library professionals use to build models. Each student picks one of three clients and makes a prediction by hand, plotting the client’s five data points and drawing a line with a ruler. Students then train a linear regression model on the same data with fit and answer the client’s question with predict. Along the way, they meet the input structure scikit-learn requires and read a real error message to fix it.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Your First Client Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide rulers, or another straightedge, for drawing prediction lines (one for each student)\n- Print copies of the Making Predictions Unit Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What tools can help me build a machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll step into the role of machine learning engineers and meet `scikit-learn`, the library professionals use to build models. "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Multiple Linear Regression",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make regression models more accurate?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about multiple linear regression as a tool for making predictions based on more than one input variable. Students are introduced to problems where making predictions based on a single variable isn’t sufficient, and they experiment with both one-variable and two-variable linear regression models to see how they compare. Then, they write Python code to train multiple linear regression models of their own.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Grocery Shopping Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make regression models more accurate?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about multiple linear regression as a tool for making predictions based on more than one input variable."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Encodings",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What does data need to look like to use machine learning?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn to prepare data for a machine learning model. They take on the role of a machine learning engineer building a feature matrix—a table of numbers used as input to a machine learning model. They encounter the challenge of including non-numeric data in a feature matrix and learn about its solution: encodings. Students then practice using two kinds of encoding—binary encoding and one-hot encoding—in a context of their choice. Along the way, students learn about `scikit-learn`’s built-in encoders.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Encodings Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "**What does data need to look like to use machine learning?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to prepare data for a machine learning model."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Loss Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do loss functions help us to compare models?**\n\nIn this lesson, students experiment with different loss functions for evaluating the quality of predictions. In addition to reviewing the role of absolute error, students are also introduced to squared error as a common way to evaluate predictions in machine learning models. Students calculate mean squared error manually on their own predictions, then write code to use mean squared error to compare two different machine learning models.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Animal Weights Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**How do loss functions help us to compare models?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll experiment with different loss functions for evaluating the quality of prediction. You'll calculate mean squared error manually on your own predictions, then write code to use mean squared error to compare two different machine learning models."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Training and Test Sets",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make sure models will work well in the real world?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to split data into training sets and test sets. They will then evaluate whether the resulting model is generalized by testing it on data not included in the training data. They will learn about how a model can achieve near zero loss on training data while being useless on new unseen data. This is called \"overfitting\", in which the model memorizes the training set rather than learning the underlying pattern.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Beach Days Activity Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make sure models will work well in the real world?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to split data into training sets and test sets. You will then evaluate whether the resulting model is generalized by testing it on data not included in the training data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Regression Project",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students visit the fictional town of Regreston, applying their knowledge of linear regression to solve a business problem of their choice. Students first meet three business owners in Regreston and practice framing their problems in terms regression can solve. Students then use data offered by the businesses to build, evaluate, and use a multiple linear regression model. Along the way, they learn about **list comprehensions**—Python syntax that simplifies building lists. By the end of the project, students have applied what they’ve learned in the unit so far: how to prepare data for, build, and test regression models.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Project Planning Guide for each student",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll visit the fictional town of Regreston, applying your knowledge of linear regression to solve a business problem of your choice."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Nearest Neighbor Classification",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What tools can help me build classifiers?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how to implement nearest neighbors classification with three or more features using `scikit-learn`. They first compare two measurements of distance—Manhattan and Euclidean distance—to understand how nearest-neighbors classifiers calculate distance across many features. Students then step into a career of their choice, using `scikit-learn` to build a nearest-neighbors classifier and solve a problem someone in their chosen career might face.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Pet Classification Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**What tools can help me build classifiers?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how to implement nearest neighbors classification with three or more features using `scikit-learn`."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Scaling Features",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why does the scale of data matter to a machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, students are introduced to feature scaling as a data preprocessing step for working with machine learning models. Students explore a nearest-neighbor classifier that works with multiple features and identify cases where the classifier might fail. Together, students work through a feature scaling process to improve the classifier’s performance. Then, students implement that logic in Python to build models that can perform more accurately.\n",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Workout Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**Why does the scale of data matter to a machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore feature scaling as a data preprocessing step for working with machine learning models. You'll then implement that logic in Python to build models that can perform more accurately.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "Evaluating Decision Trees",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What kinds of rules result in better classifiers?**\n\nIn this lesson, students revisit decision trees and consider how to evaluate whether a particular question in a decision tree does a good job of splitting data into groups. Students explore the mathematical concept of impurity as a measure of how effective a particular question is. Students practice computing impurity both by hand and by writing Python code to determine impurity from a dataset.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Water Safety Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**What kinds of rules result in better classifiers?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll revisit decision trees and consider how to evaluate whether a particular question in a decision tree does a good job of splitting data into groups. You'll explore the mathematical concept of impurity as a measure of how effective a particular question is."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Decision Tree Learning",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can a computer learn what decision tree is best?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn to use decision trees as a machine learning tool for classification. Students start by exploring an algorithm that can be used to construct a decision tree by asking a series of questions that split data into pure groups. Students build a decision tree by hand, then write Python code to build a decision tree based on training data.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the TV Preferences Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can a computer learn what decision tree is best?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn to use decision trees as a machine learning tool for classification. You'll use an algorithm to  build a decision tree by hand, then write Python code to build a decision tree based on training data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Hyperparameters",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What decisions do I need to make about my machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of game designers at a studio releasing a new video game: Lantern Deep. It’s up to students to optimize the studio’s classifiers. To do so, they learn about hyperparameters and how to tune them.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Tuning Report Activity Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What decisions do I need to make about my machine learning model?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of a game designer at a studio releasing a new video game: Lantern Deep. It’s up to you to optimize the studio’s classifiers. To do so, you'll learn about hyperparameters and how to tune them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Classification Project",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students choose a classification problem and build a classifier to solve it. They explore the shape of their chosen problem, using their findings to inform their choice of classifier: either a nearest-neighbors classifier or a decision tree. Students then build their chosen classifier: they encode features, split their data, and fit their model. Finally, students evaluate their model using metrics of their choice and optimize its performance for those metrics. Throughout the project, students reflect on their design decisions in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Classification Project Planning Guide for each student.",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll choose a classification problem and build a classifier to solve it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-14",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": true,
