      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19e3ebee-ff9f-48e5-888f-31dc981eb23d",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to work on Lesson 5, Levels 1-4.\n- **Level 1:** Students import `train_test_split` and run it to split the data\n- **Level 2:** Students fit the model on the training data\n- **Level 3:** Students test the model on the test data\n- **Level 4:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students complete the programs, look for possible areas of confusion.\n- Students may not understand how “unpacking” works. Be sure to explain this by reviewing the slide above and stressing the importance of the order of the variables.\n- If students rerun the model and get different results, explain how train_test_split randomly selects data for testing."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19e3ebee-ff9f-48e5-888f-31dc981eb23d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "25beb2b4-625b-4dd1-935d-5b5892cb27f8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfa337d4-3853-4f81-a128-9186ed17cef0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfa337d4-3853-4f81-a128-9186ed17cef0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c634d80a-b689-465a-af1d-5ad229a686f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6313af2e-a130-47c4-957a-1960a9bb18ef",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Over the next two lessons, you’ll apply what you’ve learned about building regression models in `scikit-learn`, helping a business of your choice solve a problem they’re facing.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6313af2e-a130-47c4-957a-1960a9bb18ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "622ee288-572b-40c0-9e55-7889e2e389ac",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Regression Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "622ee288-572b-40c0-9e55-7889e2e389ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "871f24da-945b-48d5-accc-84a791a11f3c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in choosing a business problem for which they’ll build their regression model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Choosing a Context"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "871f24da-945b-48d5-accc-84a791a11f3c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b287897-807f-4a26-9bd3-dc03f8e76b36",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this project, you’re learning about three businesses in the fictional town of Regreston. Your task is to frame their problem as one that can be solved with regression, build a regression model, and use your model to help them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b287897-807f-4a26-9bd3-dc03f8e76b36",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d96b0855-fb26-4d2e-b316-e5b4d833b346",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete Step 1 in their Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d96b0855-fb26-4d2e-b316-e5b4d833b346",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a6d01fd-bd4c-40d1-aa6e-1102e2f50956",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Read about each of the three businesses in Regreston. Then, choose the business you’d like to help.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a6d01fd-bd4c-40d1-aa6e-1102e2f50956",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "533921d3-bfdb-43c9-8cfa-a38f77115299",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete Step 2 in their Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "533921d3-bfdb-43c9-8cfa-a38f77115299",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adf2af6f-ebd4-4c17-8fef-631e73163463",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, frame your business’s problem in terms regression might be able to solve. Think about what a regression model could help your business predict. And think about the kinds of information it might be helpful to have to predict it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adf2af6f-ebd4-4c17-8fef-631e73163463",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9eaeb893-15fa-4824-9960-dac6dd635900",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Prompt students to begin building their regression model.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Regression Project",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If some students finish Levels 1 and 2 more quickly than others, consider allowing them to move on to the later levels. Or, prompt them to help their classmates with their projects."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This project uses many of the same patterns of code students have implemented in earlier lessons. Consider inviting students to review their saved code throughout Unit 4 to identify patterns that may be useful in this project."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9eaeb893-15fa-4824-9960-dac6dd635900",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f0629a5f-38ed-444c-8fef-f26ffa12cc4a",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Regression Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f0629a5f-38ed-444c-8fef-f26ffa12cc4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ade1d59c-51bb-49b5-8ca0-690be5e731ef",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students familiarize themselves with the dataset they’ll build their regression model with. They also start to encode the features their model will use.\n- **Level 2:** Students assemble their encoded features into a feature matrix. They also define their model’s outputs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students start to build their models, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure how to access the data given to them. Point out that data is a list of dictionaries. Each dictionary represents each row in the CSV file they’ve been given, and each dictionary’s keys are given by the CSV’s header row.\n- Students who aren’t sure which features to choose. Students can choose any features they’d like—though remind them that each feature likely has relevant information to add to their model. Point out that the key thing is to distinguish between the data they’ll use as a feature (i.e., what they’ll use to make a prediction) and the data they’ll use as a label (i.e., what they’re trying to predict)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ade1d59c-51bb-49b5-8ca0-690be5e731ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ba898fb-ff35-4f53-8c56-af79c250b949",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to list comprehensions before, during, or after Levels 1–2."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ba898fb-ff35-4f53-8c56-af79c250b949",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e200b3a3-06ba-42bc-a837-aff8fd47da24",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you work on this project, you have the opportunity to practice using a new piece of Python syntax that may make your program easier to write. Let’s explore it together.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e200b3a3-06ba-42bc-a837-aff8fd47da24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed642371-cd3a-40e5-b464-7141b74bca7d",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about the structure of the data they’re given."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed642371-cd3a-40e5-b464-7141b74bca7d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da9e4c17-4303-424b-b913-29fbe365f463",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As Level 1 says, you get each business’s data as a list of dictionaries. Here’s an example of a simple data set. Notice this list has five elements. Each is a dictionary, with keys that represent features a model could use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da9e4c17-4303-424b-b913-29fbe365f463",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "196d365c-f44a-481c-9751-936f27b9a4ad",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about how they’ve previously transformed data into encodings."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "196d365c-f44a-481c-9751-936f27b9a4ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "013128dc-0a1c-4860-8b21-fb911149ebb7",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, as in the past, you need to transform your list of data into other lists—one for each feature. Previously, you’ve built lists of features like this: starting with an empty list, you iterate over the list of data, appending elements as you go.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "013128dc-0a1c-4860-8b21-fb911149ebb7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "16c35993-5206-4775-80f3-1808a4df47d1",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students a simple list comprehension."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "16c35993-5206-4775-80f3-1808a4df47d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8730f364-05fc-4cfb-be0c-20599ff8fe2a",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The same code you just saw could be written more simply, like this. In Python, this syntax is called a list comprehension. It expresses the same ideas as the code you just saw—just in a more compact way.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8730f364-05fc-4cfb-be0c-20599ff8fe2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a5d7ce0-adfb-48ff-86ff-f7cda0ca1464",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the anatomy of a list comprehension, starting by highlighting where a list comprehension iterates over an old list."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a5d7ce0-adfb-48ff-86ff-f7cda0ca1464",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e73e1545-6283-4e99-b065-b93950aba9cd",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A list comprehension is a compact way to turn one list into another. It starts with syntax that represents iterating over each element in an old list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e73e1545-6283-4e99-b065-b93950aba9cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60a80c6e-0109-403e-a4cb-d681095cafa3",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to spot where a list comprehension transforms elements of an old list."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60a80c6e-0109-403e-a4cb-d681095cafa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9c658a4-68ae-4064-b2aa-6e5410e9625d",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Then, a list comprehension transforms each element of the old list. Here, its transforms each dictionary—row—by accessing the value of the feature we care about.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9c658a4-68ae-4064-b2aa-6e5410e9625d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bee51ec-9415-468f-93a9-56cc8571cb9b",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students how to see a list comprehension as the compact construction of a new list from an old list."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bee51ec-9415-468f-93a9-56cc8571cb9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "393fc7f1-92ad-4ecb-bbe1-95d388c296e8",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The brackets denote that we want to keep each transformed element as part of a new list.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "393fc7f1-92ad-4ecb-bbe1-95d388c296e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ba71e08-1f58-4bd0-aba4-aef98939f585",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students a list comprehension that implements binary encoding."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ba71e08-1f58-4bd0-aba4-aef98939f585",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d04d985-f852-4dc6-8760-384251c7dc03",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A list comprehension can even use conditionals. Here, a list comprehension implements binary encoding. For each row in our data, the new list will have a value of 1 if a feature value is 18 or greater and, otherwise, a value of 0.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d04d985-f852-4dc6-8760-384251c7dc03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77ab5b06-8d73-4b7d-a913-add30796de8c",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77ab5b06-8d73-4b7d-a913-add30796de8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f69bff6-8e39-44a6-bf0a-ab605e5d1971",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you come back next time, you’ll continue working on your model.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f69bff6-8e39-44a6-bf0a-ab605e5d1971",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ca434998-7ff0-42cf-a81f-fe882d63ae0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "850d8f88-f5e3-4a1a-97ec-892926d8a48a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to share their project progress with each other. Give students an opportunity to surface areas where they’ve encountered challenges in their project and encourage students to give each other suggestions.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "850d8f88-f5e3-4a1a-97ec-892926d8a48a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2cb4fe9-d644-4efb-b3f2-639e7a839e2d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in finishing their project.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Regression Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2cb4fe9-d644-4efb-b3f2-639e7a839e2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4828ea0-c2c1-42b4-977d-4ac6e8769972",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Regression Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4828ea0-c2c1-42b4-977d-4ac6e8769972",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f333defc-ba62-4032-a036-bae4dd939571",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 3–5.\n- **Level 3:** Students split their encoded data into training and test sets.\n- **Level 4:** Students create and fit a linear regression model using their training data.\n- **Level 5:** Students use mean squared error to evaluate their linear regression model against their test data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students evaluate their model using mean squared error, consider helping them interpret the values they find. Each time they run their program, they’ll get a different value for mean squared error—this is because their program randomly splits train and test data on each run. An easy way to interpret mean squared error is to take the value’s square root: this is the average difference between the model’s predicted values and actual values."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f333defc-ba62-4032-a036-bae4dd939571",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86a19f7a-9bfc-48dd-a0cc-4405e23784dd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you’ve created your model, it’s time to make a prediction with it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86a19f7a-9bfc-48dd-a0cc-4405e23784dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd89795d-6843-4e79-9640-ac04a1b90723",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce how to make a prediction with students’ models."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd89795d-6843-4e79-9640-ac04a1b90723",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f5c15fd-169c-4e17-9443-939655a7a70b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To make a prediction, remember you can use `model.predict`. It takes a list of encoded feature values, inside of another list. When you have multiple features, though, it can be tricky to understand the feature values you should include in the list. One way to remember is this: order your feature values in the same order as the columns of your feature matrix. By default, `OneHotEncoder` will create feature columns in alphabetical order. So if you one of your features had three categories—\"Bagged,\" \"Boxed,\", and \"Wrapped\"—you can imagine three columns, each representing one of those categories, in alphabetical order.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f5c15fd-169c-4e17-9443-939655a7a70b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8edd1e71-b57a-4587-8e93-98758284621d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Regression Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8edd1e71-b57a-4587-8e93-98758284621d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e80c36e2-a02c-4631-b44f-a720996295dd",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 6–7.\n- **Level 6:** Students use their model to make a prediction.\n- **Level 7:** Students complete a reflection about the accuracy of their model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on their model, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure how to use their model to make a prediction. Guide them in interpreting their feature matrix to assess the order in which they should pass their feature values.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If some students finish earlier than others, consider inviting them to help out other classmates. Or, prompt very early finishers to choose another business and build another model."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e80c36e2-a02c-4631-b44f-a720996295dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c199dbd-dd4b-4594-9882-8218e9ae5e99",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c199dbd-dd4b-4594-9882-8218e9ae5e99",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1bab34f5-1775-4908-ad1d-9884c70d07e5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bbda9e8-ace8-44a0-9b8d-08744674c1c3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In your last project, you used regression to predict numbers. Today we’ll be working with nearest neighbor classification to predict labels, like \"late\" or \"on time.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bbda9e8-ace8-44a0-9b8d-08744674c1c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f084de37-fecc-4ddb-a5b5-9016613cac3f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ac2ccba4-3610-4fe9-86d8-4c365b12892c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students that in Unit 2, they worked with nearest neighbor classifiers by visualizing data and looking for nearest neighbors.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *We can easily visualize nearest neighbors when there are two features. How would the visualization need to change if we had more?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider what nearest neighbors would look like with three or more features. Strong responses will suggest that the visualization itself would need to become three-dimensional—or at least have a way to visualize three dimensions on a two-dimensional screen. Strong responses may also suggest that there isn’t a practical way to visualize more than three dimensions and that, at some point, it may be more useful to use something like a table. If students struggle to answer this question, consider reminding them that each of the two features corresponds to one axis of the plot they see. Ask: \"What, then, would need to happen if we added a third feature?\".\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ac2ccba4-3610-4fe9-86d8-4c365b12892c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f084de37-fecc-4ddb-a5b5-9016613cac3f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0904838b-804b-4a2a-a012-986034707949",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to methods of calculating distance in many dimensions and guide them in applying these distance calculations in the context of nearest neighbors classification.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students a nearest neighbors classification problem. Prompt students to guess whether the unknown pet is a cat or a dog.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Distance in Many Dimensions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0904838b-804b-4a2a-a012-986034707949",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ca81382-4fca-44f2-928b-8e47ff2bf30c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s look at trying to classify cats and dogs. Notice each has three features: height, weight and tail length. We want to determine whether the unknown pet is a cat or a dog, using nearest neighbors. Can you tell which the unknown pet is?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ca81382-4fca-44f2-928b-8e47ff2bf30c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e01b7bbf-e751-4e43-9859-5944e77e94ac",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Let students discuss for a moment. Accept a few guesses, then continue on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce distance calculation as a prerequisite for solving the classification problem."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e01b7bbf-e751-4e43-9859-5944e77e94ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d2c3a66-e613-422d-b096-508c6d8f01d3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To decide whether the unknown pet is a cat or a dog, you would need to calculate its distance from the two known pets.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d2c3a66-e613-422d-b096-508c6d8f01d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02ded92d-154c-4daf-aaef-c1f1117bbbff",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the idea that there are multiple ways to calculate distance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02ded92d-154c-4daf-aaef-c1f1117bbbff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fab7bb0-6510-420e-987c-1e63634f06a9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It turns out, though, that there are multiple ways to calculate distance.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fab7bb0-6510-420e-987c-1e63634f06a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c1aa6d4-d69a-42ce-8740-6765af77c298",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you calculate the distance between the unknown pet and each of the known pets?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share the strategy they used to calculate distance. It’s most likely that students have summed distances across each feature: that is, added up the distance in height, the distance in weight, and the distance in tail length. When students share their thinking, make clear that they’re sharing one way to calculate distance—but that there are also others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Manhattan distance*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c1aa6d4-d69a-42ce-8740-6765af77c298",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "556042e5-ec24-44f4-ad1b-afd7b1a6c00f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One way to calculate distance is to sum the distances for each feature. That is, to add the distance in height, the distance in weight, and the distance in tail length. This is called the Manhattan distance. It’s so named because, to navigate Manhattan, you need to travel down adjacent roads—you can’t cut through buildings.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "556042e5-ec24-44f4-ad1b-afd7b1a6c00f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39f21d40-9ef4-4813-902b-5fa33b47f9a8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *Euclidean distance*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39f21d40-9ef4-4813-902b-5fa33b47f9a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e4ace27-b8aa-466c-9029-806a83d7e9af",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Another way to calculate distance is the Euclidean distance. This is the length of a straight line between two points.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e4ace27-b8aa-466c-9029-806a83d7e9af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd2138a6-6132-4b11-bc7d-a420b9477a3b",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the formula to calculate Euclidean distance."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd2138a6-6132-4b11-bc7d-a420b9477a3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e4fa38b-dd31-49b4-a99f-509f1e6a47ba",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To find the Euclidean distance, you can use this formula. You square the difference between each feature, add those squares together, then take the square root of that sum.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e4fa38b-dd31-49b4-a99f-509f1e6a47ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1fd6b23-af46-41d4-b0c1-1ef4742484ff",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll now have a chance to test out these two ways of calculating distance.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1fd6b23-af46-41d4-b0c1-1ef4742484ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c8d02f1-9639-41c9-9baf-417e70c422b7",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Pet Classification Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to calculate the Manhattan and Euclidean distances between the unknown pet and the cat and the dog.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students begin calculating the distances, clarify any misunderstandings.\n- Some students might be unsure what absolute value means. Remind students that when measuring Manhattan distance, they care about the non-negative number difference between each feature value: a feature that’s 2 away and a feature that’s -2 away are equivalent from a distance perspective.\n- Some students may be unsure how to apply the distance formula. Remind them to take the square root of the sum of the squared differences for each dimension."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c8d02f1-9639-41c9-9baf-417e70c422b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e250a81b-07ae-4d86-8aea-691c53725ba3",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What did you notice about the two sets of calculations? Were they the same? Did they affect the prediction? Do you think they would always predict the same category?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e250a81b-07ae-4d86-8aea-691c53725ba3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c057e90-7af5-42d6-b1c8-fecf5650c6c1",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take one or two student responses to reflect on the calculations students performed in the activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c057e90-7af5-42d6-b1c8-fecf5650c6c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8065ca08-4ebe-4c06-b325-da8e9968faea",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we had 100 points of training data, we would have to do this calculation 100 times! This is why we use the tools in `scikit-learn`, like `KNeighborsClassifier`, to do this work for us.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- There are different ways to calculate distance, and engineers will sometimes use one or the other. Euclidean is more common when the features represent physical space, while Manhattan is more common when features represent different kinds of things.\n- Note  that `scikit-learn` uses Euclidean distance by default, but it can be configured to use Manhattan distance. The distance measure is used for predictions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8065ca08-4ebe-4c06-b325-da8e9968faea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85a0677f-aded-4fa3-85e2-9b2e89c4cfb6",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students use `scikit-learn` to train a `KNeighborsClassifier` and use the trained model to classify an unknown pet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the code for training a nearest-neighbors classifier and explain what each part is doing. The code assumes that the `train_inputs`, `train_outputs`, `test_inputs`, and `test_ouputs` variables are already defined.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Implementing Nearest Neighbors Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85a0677f-aded-4fa3-85e2-9b2e89c4cfb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b906edec-14f6-43a4-a654-0aa7f372f492",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here is an example of how to use `KNeighborsClassifier` to determine if the unknown pet is a cat or a dog. We configure the classifier with `n_neighbors=1` on line 3, so that when we call `model.predict` next, the classifier will classify the new point based on a single nearest neighbor. `model.fit` trains the model. One line 6, we score the model to see how accurate it is on the test data, and we can use `model.predict` to predict if the unknown pet is a cat or a dog.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "`predict` always returns a list of answers, one for each object we ask about. We asked about just one object, so the list has one item. The `[0]` goes after the parentheses because it is applied to the result that `predict` hands back. It means \"give me the item at position 0 (the first item) of that list\", which removes the brackets and quotes when we print."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b906edec-14f6-43a4-a654-0aa7f372f492",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2f3d37a-85b3-4e16-83cc-e65d70abfdb2",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the coding activity, where students will take the role of a professional who needs to make a classification decision."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2f3d37a-85b3-4e16-83cc-e65d70abfdb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54d86cb9-aa91-45c9-8051-e7265ade8954",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you will get a chance to practice using the `scikit-learn` `KNeighborsClassifier`.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54d86cb9-aa91-45c9-8051-e7265ade8954",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8b2e9d6-23bd-43d7-b7a2-de6ce6bf9267",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Nearest Neighbor Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8b2e9d6-23bd-43d7-b7a2-de6ce6bf9267",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9795dfb-5dec-476f-a2db-998e39159085",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9795dfb-5dec-476f-a2db-998e39159085",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa2cb4d6-2c22-4839-88eb-050d61c5eeff",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-3.\n- **Level 1:** Students configure their classifier with the setting `n_neighbors=1`, using `KNeighborsClassifier`. They will then train the model using `model.fit`.\n- **Level 2:** Building upon the code students wrote in Level 1, students test their model using `model.score`. Then, students classify a new unknown input.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students complete the programs, look for possible areas of confusion.\n- Students may not understand how `n_neighbors=1` affects the classifier. Remind students that nearest neighbor classifiers can differ in how many neighbors they look at when determining what category an input belongs to. In Lesson 9, students will explore this idea in more detail.\n- If students re-run their code and get different results, remind students that `train_test_split` randomly selects data for testing. Each run might result in a slightly different outcome.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa2cb4d6-2c22-4839-88eb-050d61c5eeff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f3146d3-9d0f-4431-9ad5-a64c5e950147"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b6c824a-b35d-49c6-baae-643a23e9a892",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b6c824a-b35d-49c6-baae-643a23e9a892",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3c76a720-67ca-4f4d-8250-0ad0c8effe11"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07c27e9c-d678-423d-a92c-a2a17a88e967",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario that students will explore in the first part of the activity. Imagine building an app that needs to decide if a workout is a run or bicycle ride. Point out to students that this is an example of a classification problem: the input is information about the workout and the output is whether the workout is a run or a bicycle ride.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** With students, work through the process of identifying whether the first workout or the second workout is the nearest neighbor to the third workout. Invite students to use a sheet of paper to write down their calculations. You can keep track of relevant information on the board as you calculate together.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07c27e9c-d678-423d-a92c-a2a17a88e967",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5facb337-0cda-4ffb-b52d-664f5d2b45be",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To measure the “distance” between any two workouts, we’ll calculate the difference between their elevation changes, the difference between their speeds, the difference between their heart rates, and add the values up.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5facb337-0cda-4ffb-b52d-664f5d2b45be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34b7ef49-fb58-4278-8f97-b945e14c8ed2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to pull out a calculator, or distribute calculators to students. Students will likely find a calculator helpful for the activities throughout this lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to compute the distance between the third workout and the run. After students have had a minute or so to work, call on a student to explain their answer. The difference between elevation changes is 10, the difference between speeds is 1, the difference between heart rate is 10, so the total distance is the sum: 21.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to compute the distance between the third workout and the bicycle ride.  After students have had a minute or so to work, call on a student to explain their answer. The difference between elevation changes is 20, the difference between speeds is 6, the difference between heart rate is 30, so the total distance is the sum: 56."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34b7ef49-fb58-4278-8f97-b945e14c8ed2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31c51fdd-699c-46d6-b05d-86e241a1e5a8",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The distance to the run is smaller than the distance to the bicycle ride. As a result, a nearest-neighbor classifier might conclude that the third workout is a run.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31c51fdd-699c-46d6-b05d-86e241a1e5a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4e77ee5-d13d-461a-b4fa-4c23f0a10e1a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Confirm that the third workout is, in fact, a run."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4e77ee5-d13d-461a-b4fa-4c23f0a10e1a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88ab4dd9-ff73-464f-9ef5-a5a65ff431b7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The classifier worked well this time, but today we’ll explore some cases where classifiers like this can run into problems that we need to solve.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In the last lesson, students were introduced to both Manhattan and Euclidean distance metrics. For simplicity, in this example, students use the Manhattan distance to compute distances.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88ab4dd9-ff73-464f-9ef5-a5a65ff431b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d08dfdc6-2b00-4d2e-a41d-4bc4a80861f3",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d08dfdc6-2b00-4d2e-a41d-4bc4a80861f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f619c922-a619-43ad-a95d-2e3997a97cbd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de00084d-8332-4bcf-bbfe-2a1b51f1e55d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students work through an example together to motivate the need for feature scaling in machine learning models.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Introducing Feature Scaling"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de00084d-8332-4bcf-bbfe-2a1b51f1e55d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e8f058a-63c5-41c2-b662-3554939c6638",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s try another example to see how this nearest-neighbor classifier might work.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e8f058a-63c5-41c2-b662-3554939c6638",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69871867-fa00-447a-bec8-f2c93fd35530",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to work in partners or small groups to determine whether this new third workout has a shorter distance to the first workout or to the second workout. They should calculate distance by measuring how far apart two workouts are on each of the three features and then add those differences together.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a few minutes to do the calculations on their own, then ask for volunteers to talk through the calculation process. The distance from the third workout to the run is 5 + 10 + 10 = 25. The distance from the third workout to the bicycle ride is 25 + 5 + 10 = 40. The distance is shorter to the run, so the algorithm would classify the third workout as a run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that the third workout was in fact a bicycle ride. (Intuitively, the speed might be a hint that this is a case.) Note to students that this time, the nearest-neighbor classifier got the answer wrong: the workout should have been classified as a bicycle ride, but it was classified as a run instead.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What went wrong with our algorithm? What could we do to make it better?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students talk through why the classifier made a mistake in this case. The main insight this discussion is meant to reveal is that the “Speed” features is out-of-balance with the other features. A strong response will notice that speed is measured in single or double-digit numbers, while elevation change and heart rate are measured in triple-digit numbers, so the elevation change and heart rate values end up mattering much more than the speed feature when determining nearest neighbors. Students’ proposed solutions might vary, but could be any approach that helps treat each feature equally. Students don’t need to have a precise mathematical answer for how to do that yet: the goal of the discussion is just to help students recognize the problem. There are multiple other reasons for the classifier’s failure students could suggest that would technically be accurate: for example, the training data only has one example from each category and could benefit from more data, or that having access to more features might help the classifier better tell the workouts apart. If students suggest these ideas, acknowledge their validity but encourage them to think about how the algorithm could have been improved even with the existing data as-is."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69871867-fa00-447a-bec8-f2c93fd35530",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d2b1c508-0848-4cd6-b0fc-9d9f79f78bec",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Because the scale of the speed number is usually smaller than the elevation change and heart rate numbers, the nearest-neighbor classifier ends up not caring about speed as much. What we want is some way to help our classifier care about all of the features.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d2b1c508-0848-4cd6-b0fc-9d9f79f78bec",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1054beef-b9e7-4c06-98ef-7047310bdd4e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _feature scaling_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1054beef-b9e7-4c06-98ef-7047310bdd4e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc15e1a8-f6bd-4754-85f6-9ebde1ddb9a0",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Data features can have very different scales: some features are larger than others, and some features span a wider range than others. When that happens, we can use feature scaling to make sure that features use a similar scale. This lets our algorithms work with different features more effectively.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc15e1a8-f6bd-4754-85f6-9ebde1ddb9a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65484552-637f-457f-9f09-6f50a2bb0dc9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce min-max scaling as a way to scale features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the algorithm for min-max scaling. The algorithm involves first finding the minimum and maximum values for a feature in the training data. Then, a formula allows translating any number into the scaled version of that number."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65484552-637f-457f-9f09-6f50a2bb0dc9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e793dee-d5dc-4c0c-b0bf-117524fddf2d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll now have a chance to try scaling a dataset so that it will work better in AI models.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e793dee-d5dc-4c0c-b0bf-117524fddf2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0232bd15-f6f5-4f27-9eeb-b9c71b3cef77",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Workouts Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the first feature in the activity guide together."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0232bd15-f6f5-4f27-9eeb-b9c71b3cef77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8e40f1c-f119-436c-891c-963d75346191",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s try to use min-max scaling on our dataset of workouts so that it will work better with our classification algorithms. We’ll start by scaling the elevation change feature.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8e40f1c-f119-436c-891c-963d75346191",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "534f91e8-506f-4b1f-ae95-7e2ae6f501e5",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Start with the “Elevation Change” column. Call on students to identify the minimum and maximum value for the elevation change feature.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Confirm that 200 is the minimum value and 320 is the maximum value.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the formula for scaling features: take each value, subtract the minimum, then divide by the difference between the maximum and the minimum. The slide shows a version of the formula with the minimum and maximum values for elevation change substituted.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to pick one of the four elevation change numbers and scale it according to the min-max scaling formula visible on the slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a minute or so to determine the scaled value. Then, for each workout, ask a student to offer the scaled result. The correct answers should be: 0.00, 1.00, 0.58, and 0.25 (rounded to the nearest two decimal places).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the scaled values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what they notice about the scaled values. Help students identify that all values are between 0 and 1, that the smallest value from the dataset was assigned a scaled value of 0, that the largest value from the dataset was assigned a scaled value of 1, and that each value stays in the same order relative to the others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the rest of the Workouts Activity Guide in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the activity guide, keep an eye out for the following:\n- Students who aren’t sure how to apply the scaling formula. First, work with students to identify the minimum and maximum values for each feature. Then, walk through how to substitute the appropriate values into the formula to compute the scaled results for each feature. Verify that students are using the appropriate minimum and maximum value for each feature.\n- When students work with the new data, make sure they use the minimum and maximum value from the training data, not the minimum and maximum value from the new data. Help students see that when testing a model trained with scaled data, the test data needs to be scaled in exactly the same way as the original training data, otherwise the test data will have a different meaning than what the model was trained to work with."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "534f91e8-506f-4b1f-ae95-7e2ae6f501e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1769b361-c494-4dd3-8a40-ca9ecd0ff5ce",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students use `scikit-learn` to scale features and use those features to train a machine learning model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce how `scikit-learn` can be used to scale features. Point out to students that feature scaling is a pre-processing step: features are scaled before they’re provided as inputs to a machine learning model. Line 5 creates a new scaler. Line 6 “fits” the scaler to the training data (in other words, determines what the minimum and maximum values for each feature are) and then transforms the training input values into their scaled versions. Then line 9 uses the already-fitted scaler to apply the same transformation to the test data.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Implementing Feature Scaling"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1769b361-c494-4dd3-8a40-ca9ecd0ff5ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a6ee012-b802-49af-ae97-1df592d5023b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We use the `fit_transform` method on the training data so that scaler learns what minimum and maximum values to use and then transforms the data into its scaled version. Then, we use the `transform` method so that the test data is scaled in exactly the same way as the training data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a6ee012-b802-49af-ae97-1df592d5023b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3efcbd31-cd89-46c5-9f3c-38eef75fb281",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students an opportunity to ask questions about the code for feature scaling.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, you could consider inviting students to think more deeply about why the test data must be scaled using the minimum and maximum values derived from the training data. Help students see that if a machine learning model is trained on scaled data, then to use the model effectively, the test data must be scaled in the same way. Otherwise, the model would be working with data that has a different meaning than the data that it was trained on.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3efcbd31-cd89-46c5-9f3c-38eef75fb281",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9909e9c-31f6-4faa-ab1a-6874afe79f6a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Scale Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9909e9c-31f6-4faa-ab1a-6874afe79f6a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4aa86197-1fcb-4c20-b22a-d5595ab3531c",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4aa86197-1fcb-4c20-b22a-d5595ab3531c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a5a3840-7f47-4d5f-9a2c-480bb28cf208",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students update a nearest-neighbor classifier to use feature scaling.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students to reflect on their model’s performance and argue for why the performance changes due to feature scaling.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students implement feature scaling, look for common areas of confusion.\n- Students might need help with the syntax for using a `MinMaxScaler`. Show the previous slide again if needed, and help students to create a new `MinMaxScaler` and call its methods.\n- Students might be confused about the difference between `fit_transform` and `transform`. Remind students that the former both “fits” the scaler (that is, teaches it specifically what minimum/maximum values to use) and “transforms” the data (converts the data to use the scaled versions). The latter only transforms the data. When working with training data, it’s important to fit and transform the data; but when working with test data, students should only transform the data (the scaler has already been fit to use the minimum/maximum values from the training data).\n- An easy mistake for students to make is forgetting to use the scaled data both when training the model and testing the model. If students train their model with scaled data but test it with unscaled data, their model’s accuracy will likely be low: keep an eye out for this possibility if students are getting unexpected results.\n- It’s possible that students will see slightly different results each time they run the program. This is because the splitting of training and test data happens randomly. You might ask students to run the code multiple times to see whether the behavior they see is consistent.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand the impact that feature scaling has on the behavior of machine learning models. Effective responses should identify the different scales of features in the data students are working with, noting that a nearest-neighbor classifier on unscaled data might end up only focusing on the largest features. If students struggle with understanding the impact of feature scaling, it might be helpful to make the distance computation concrete by looking at the slide with both unscaled and scaled data. Talk with students about how a nearest-neighbor classifier would behave when computing distances based on unscaled data versus computing distances based on scaled data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a5a3840-7f47-4d5f-9a2c-480bb28cf208",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7cba2825-428e-4659-841d-63a48f5fe6f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f91bad8f-7749-4641-9068-723878cb7ac2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f91bad8f-7749-4641-9068-723878cb7ac2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "afea0933-a31c-4155-aa03-b9682357b4c9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c90a3f12-be0f-459c-af22-3e9b3f5141c7",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for the first part of today’s lesson. Students will play the role of data analysts at a water treatment plant, responsible for determining if samples of water are safe to drink.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c90a3f12-be0f-459c-af22-3e9b3f5141c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd4823c6-c866-469b-93cc-81e92c0169bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf78d101-899c-42be-872c-0483f83c3e6d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine that your team at the water treatment plant needs to look at each sample of water and decide on a safety rating: low risk or high risk.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf78d101-899c-42be-872c-0483f83c3e6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd4823c6-c866-469b-93cc-81e92c0169bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a36c2bf1-632e-4394-953d-d9ddd3e1f85e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What patterns do you notice in the data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start to explore patterns in the data to try to determine “rules” for whether a water sample is low risk or high risk. The patterns are subtle and there are only eight samples of data here, so students aren’t expected to identify patterns with certainty, but they should still look for possible ideas that could be worth investigating. For example, some students might notice that samples with high chlorine levels might be higher risk (the two samples with chlorine values of 4.3 and 4.1 are both high-risk). Other students might notice that sample with the highest pH (8.9) is high risk, and there are similar samples with a lower pH that are low risk, so pH values that exceed a certain threshold might be high risk too. Students might also identify that cloudiness does seem to increase risk in most cases: the average cloudiness among the high-risk samples is higher than the average cloudiness among the low-risk samples. If students aren’t sure of a pattern, encourage students to point out anything they observe in the data: the goal isn’t to get an exact answer for what defines low-risk or high-risk water samples, the goal is just to get students thinking about rules that might be useful for performing classifications."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a36c2bf1-632e-4394-953d-d9ddd3e1f85e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd4823c6-c866-469b-93cc-81e92c0169bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc343a49-6466-4316-b625-62e0b8989c46",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we wanted to build a classifier to identify low-risk and high-risk water samples, we could use a nearest-neighbor classifier that would look for the closest water sample. But we could also try to figure out general rules that can help us perform classifications. For example, a rule like “chlorine values that are more than 4 are high risk” could be a good rule. That’s what we’ll explore today.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask and are curious, pH is measured on a 0-14 scale, cloudiness is measured in NTU (Nephelometric Turbidity Units), and chlorine is measured in mg/L. Students can complete the discussion and the activity without knowing the specific units for each.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc343a49-6466-4316-b625-62e0b8989c46",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd4823c6-c866-469b-93cc-81e92c0169bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d361f34a-195b-413e-924f-b86f9529d7d0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d361f34a-195b-413e-924f-b86f9529d7d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd4823c6-c866-469b-93cc-81e92c0169bc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "26c7a8b7-0138-4a6e-97a5-a32b65c4bb95",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to impurity as a metric for evaluating whether a question in a decision tree is doing a good job of splitting data into groups. Students practice computing impurity for a group.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about decision trees, which they explored in Unit 1. A decision tree is a type of classifier. The decision tree asks questions and eventually picks a category based on the answers to those questions.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Measuring Impurity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "26c7a8b7-0138-4a6e-97a5-a32b65c4bb95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d6fe477-f17a-4df8-bf15-464a871e9a3b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we last looked at decision trees, you built decision trees based on your own intuition. But how could you or a computer know if the rules in a decision tree are good or not? Today, we’ll explore ways to measure that.\n\nSay we wanted to build a decision tree to classify water samples as high-risk or low-risk. Here are two example decision trees that each have only one question.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d6fe477-f17a-4df8-bf15-464a871e9a3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd879990-59f7-4f4a-a73f-0b6fac0eb9d9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the two decision trees. For each answer to a decision tree’s question, students can see the results for how many examples in the training data are high-risk or low-risk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which question is more useful for a classifier?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students see that Decision Tree 1 is much more useful for building a classifier. Strong responses will identify that the question in Decision Tree 1 does a pretty good job of splitting the data into high-risk and low-risk: when the answer is yes, most of the data is high-risk; when the answer is no, most of the data is low-risk. The same could be said for Decision Tree 2, but the split is less clean: each half of the decision tree still has a closer to even amount of high-risk and low-risk examples. If students struggle to identify why Decision Tree 1 does a better job, ask them how confident they would feel in saying that a water sample was high-risk if the answer to the decision tree’s question was “yes”. If students have trouble interpreting the data, explain how to interpret the high-risk and low-risk numbers present beneath each answer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd879990-59f7-4f4a-a73f-0b6fac0eb9d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08f98072-fa64-4973-801c-79dc9adcbdbe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When a question in a decision tree leads to groups that are very mixed between different categories, the question is probably not that helpful. But if a question leads to groups that have a lot of values from just one category, the question is helping us figure out that category is probably a good prediction.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might wonder about whether a decision tree that asks more than one question would be better. In general, that’s true. Today, students will focus on evaluating individual questions in a decision tree, but an effective decision tree will usually combine multiple questions to improve accuracy.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08f98072-fa64-4973-801c-79dc9adcbdbe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9946a2eb-d3ef-43a3-af91-4c07de05e749",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _impurity_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9946a2eb-d3ef-43a3-af91-4c07de05e749",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d7966ab-7fb6-4671-b363-16949b540374",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Impurity lets us measure whether a dataset has a lot of mixed-up categories or if it’s mostly one category. We want questions in a decision tree that lead to a low impurity.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Another popular way to measure the how mixed-together a dataset’s categories is to measure entropy. This lesson focuses on impurity rather than entropy for mathematical simplicity.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d7966ab-7fb6-4671-b363-16949b540374",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f789bb4-7cc5-4267-9310-ced3b571c343",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the formula for measuring impurity. Take a dataset and count what proportion of the dataset belongs to Category 1. Then count what proportion of the dataset belongs to Category 2. Take the squares of each proportion and subtract them from 1 to get the impurity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** To make the formula more concrete, you can demonstrate an example on the board and walk through it with students. Say there’s a dataset where 30% of the data belongs to Category 1 and 70% of the data belongs to Category 2. Then the impurity would be 1 - (0.3)^2 - (0.7)^2 = 0.42.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The specific of impurity measured here is known as Gini impurity. Some students might ask what happens if there are more than two categories: in that case, continue to subtract the square of the proportion of each additional category.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f789bb4-7cc5-4267-9310-ced3b571c343",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c74f044-c9ef-4a84-af1e-7c360cde79f3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students some examples of impurity values. Students should notice that when all of the data falls in one group (the top row and the bottom row of the table), impurity is 0. When the data is more mixed up, the impurity value increases.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students that the goal is for a group to have low impurity (also described as high purity). When a group has high purity, it’s easy to predict the category to which a member of the group belongs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c74f044-c9ef-4a84-af1e-7c360cde79f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6922b90f-6d4f-4bcc-8b4a-4338acb6c13d",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll try applying this formula so that you can verify for yourself which question does a better job of classifying water samples.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6922b90f-6d4f-4bcc-8b4a-4338acb6c13d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a973374e-780c-42c9-9032-3e1e0a49fc08",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Water Safety Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work through the Water Safety Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the guide, help them with the logic for computing impurity.\n- For students who aren’t sure how to determine the high risk count and the low risk count, help them interpret the training data table. For example, to determine the high risk count for “Yes” in the chlorine split, students should find the rows of the training data table for which the chlorine value is “Yes” (there are two such rows) and add together the two high risk counts.\n- For students who don’t know how to fill in values for the proportions, remind them that proportions should always be between 0 and 1 and can be computed by dividing their “High Risk Count” or “Low Risk Count” value by the “Total Count” value.\n- Encourage students to use calculators for computing the impurity value and for computing proportions where the denominator doesn’t lend itself to simple mental math.\n- A potential source of confusion is that students compute two impurity values for each question. This is because each question splits the data into two groups: each group has its own impurity value that must be calculated separately.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students who are thinking particularly critically about this exercise might ask what would happen if a question resulted in two groups where one group had a higher impurity (compared to another question) and another group had a lower impurity (compared to that same other question). This is an important observation: in general, decision tree classifiers will want a question where both groups have lower impurity, so they might take a weighted average of the two impurity values (weighted by the size of each group). This nuance isn’t explored in this activity guide for simplicity, but is an interesting discussion topic for students feeling more comfortable with the material.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a973374e-780c-42c9-9032-3e1e0a49fc08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aac785d5-ed74-4dcf-b5a6-dcbbf49794c0",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students write Python code to work with a dataset and use it to compute impurity.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Computing Impurity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aac785d5-ed74-4dcf-b5a6-dcbbf49794c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99509a23-393f-4683-9771-c8850135aaa6",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Evaluate Impurity"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99509a23-393f-4683-9771-c8850135aaa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d11c404-96b5-44f4-8de3-ccc78ea54f2a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d11c404-96b5-44f4-8de3-ccc78ea54f2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08e5eeee-2a9c-450e-9844-545a45dc491d",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students write Python code to compute the impurity of a group.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students about the importance of impurity in the context of decision trees.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through computing impurity, help them with common places where they might get stuck.\n- Students might struggle with counting the number of high risk and low risk entries. Since the data is organized as a list of dictionaries, help students think about the logic for iterating over the list. When they do, each iteration will give them a dictionary to work with, and students can write a conditional statement that accesses values in a dictionary to decide how to update the high-risk and low-risk variables.\n- Some students might not realize they need to check two different values in the dictionary. They need to consider both the pH value in the dictionary as well as the value that corresponds to which decision they’re choosing to explore (chlorine or pH).\n- Students might not know how to square a number in Python. The example solution uses Python’s exponentiation syntax (`**2`), but students aren’t expected to be familiar with this syntax. Instead, remind students that squaring a number is equivalent to multiplying it by itself, which students do know how to write in Python.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask whether machine learning engineers in practice need to manually calculate impurity, you can note that machine learning libraries will often do the calculation automatically when training certain kinds of models. Students will see an example of this in the next lesson.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students the relevance of impurity when it comes to building classifiers. Strong responses will identify that for a computer to know whether a decision tree is good or not, it needs to evaluate how well it splits data into groups, and groups are more reliable for making predictions when there’s less mixing of different categories.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08e5eeee-2a9c-450e-9844-545a45dc491d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d6a36d98-6b67-4626-9990-9075f95712fa"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa9d0c30-6fea-43d4-9fec-e5aa3a830ace",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa9d0c30-6fea-43d4-9fec-e5aa3a830ace",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ecfe213a-de5d-4ad7-97c7-41cdb02a00f5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d5436b6-9d55-4071-955f-0881df24c568",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Your friend suggests a TV show for you to watch. What questions would you ask to decide if you think you’ll like it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students start thinking about features they would use to solve a prediction problem. The number of possible answers is large: encourage students to think about what’s important to them and what kinds of features they would pay attention to. Examples of possible questions include questions that ask about any of the following: episode runtime (some people prefer shorter episodes), genre, live action vs. animation, number of seasons, number of episodes, cast size, TV network or streaming platform. Take a few ideas from students and then move on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d5436b6-9d55-4071-955f-0881df24c568",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75ca16f7-86b3-4b35-860b-2880aec85042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "856eae33-ec55-4d6e-884f-415a0c5efc26",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Whether or not you’ll like a TV show can be a classification problem: the input is information about the TV show and the output is a label that predicts whether you will or won’t like the show. Today, we’ll think about how a computer algorithm could figure out how to ask questions like the ones you just asked to make a prediction.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "856eae33-ec55-4d6e-884f-415a0c5efc26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75ca16f7-86b3-4b35-860b-2880aec85042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd9e9191-2b19-4344-8c30-ab37131b1c2c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd9e9191-2b19-4344-8c30-ab37131b1c2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "75ca16f7-86b3-4b35-860b-2880aec85042"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0708648a-863a-4afb-b436-98ca7f3a1331",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students build on their understanding of decision trees and impurity to consider an algorithm for learning decision trees. Students work through an activity where they build a decision tree by following the algorithm.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _decision tree learning_.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Introducing Decision Tree Learning"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0708648a-863a-4afb-b436-98ca7f3a1331",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "866c6208-c36f-48db-9c4c-0a71e9c05b94",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve seen how decision trees can be used to make predictions. Today, we’ll look at an algorithm that computers can use to build a decision tree that can make predictions well.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "866c6208-c36f-48db-9c4c-0a71e9c05b94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b642e0e-d773-4faa-b21e-ce5e52d4756e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show an example of a decision tree. This decision tree represents a classification task, where circles and squares represent the two categories. In this decision tree, the initial question splits the data into two sub-groups: one with all circles and one with all squares.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students about what purity and impurity mean in the context of a dataset: they measure how mixed-up the data is. A “pure” dataset contains only data from one category. An “impure” dataset contains data from multiple categories.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b642e0e-d773-4faa-b21e-ce5e52d4756e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f734d26a-f47d-4355-a3bc-78348a3d2d49",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we build a decision tree, we want the computer to look for questions that will result in groups that are pure. This decision tree has a question that splits the data into two completely pure groups: each group has items that are from only one category.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f734d26a-f47d-4355-a3bc-78348a3d2d49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0d62817e-578a-4f8c-80d7-256f453efa68",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show an example of a decision tree where the two groups are not totally pure. In this case, the branch on the right is pure, but the branch on the left has high impurity because it mixes together both colors. This question alone isn’t good enough to distinguish between the two categories."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0d62817e-578a-4f8c-80d7-256f453efa68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e0acabc-aaff-40d5-88e0-f56f4dc4a0fb",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We won’t always have a single question that can perfectly split up the data. What do you think a computer should do if it tries to build a decision tree but the question doesn’t result in perfectly pure groups?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e0acabc-aaff-40d5-88e0-f56f4dc4a0fb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b804a9bf-ea60-4054-93db-994b1646b37d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take a few suggestions from students for possible strategies."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b804a9bf-ea60-4054-93db-994b1646b37d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca49252b-58cd-465b-9ef7-86c0dc8725a9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If we have a decision tree where the groups aren’t all pure yet, we can add another question to the tree to split the data again. The computer can pick the question that will result in the highest purity.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca49252b-58cd-465b-9ef7-86c0dc8725a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "30b3ae67-c401-46a1-8229-256ad894cbc1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce an algorithm that computers can use to build a decision tree. The algorithm starts with an impure dataset (a mix of different categories). It chooses the question that splits the data into groups that are as pure as possible, creating a new branch of the decision tree for each answer. For the dataset in each new branch, if the group isn’t perfectly pure yet, the process repeats."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "30b3ae67-c401-46a1-8229-256ad894cbc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "402ccca9-bd72-46f5-8999-947e51ded095",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Before, you built decision trees based on intuition. Now, we have an algorithm that we can give to a computer, and the computer could follow the algorithm to figure out how to build a decision tree on its own.\n\nNow, you’ll have a chance to try this algorithm and use it to build a decision tree, just like a computer would.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "402ccca9-bd72-46f5-8999-947e51ded095",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "215852f9-0322-4454-ac7f-8e6d9bb0c6d8",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the TV Preferences Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work through the TV Preferences Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the guide, help them with the logic for constructing their decision tree.\n- Students start by choosing the first question for their decision tree. The activity guide provides them with three candidate questions to consider. (In practice, a computer would consider more than just these three questions, but for time’s sake the activity picks a few questions that are worth exploring.) Students should use the training data to count up how often a television show is liked or disliked depending on the answer to each question. For students who are confused about what they are doing in this step and why, remind them that the purpose of a question in the decision tree is to use the answer to split the data into groups that help make a prediction. In this step, students are trying to evaluate whether the answer does a good job of splitting the television shows into likes and dislikes.\n- Students then need to pick which first question does the best job separating data into pure and impure groups. Some students might want to use the impurity formula from the previous lesson. It’s okay if students do this, though it’s not necessary in this case: you can encourage students to look at their results and pick which one looks like it’s the most pure. Help students see that the “Is the show a comedy?” question does a particularly good job of splitting the data: if the answer is yes, the viewer definitely likes the show. If the answer is no, the viewer probably dislikes the show, though there is one show in the training data where the viewer still liked it. None of the other questions do as good a job of splitting the data into pure groups.\n- Then, help students draw their decision trees and add a new question to complete the tree. Students might not be sure how and where to add an additional question. Help these students see which group is pure and which group is still impure: in this case, the impure group is the one where the show is not a comedy: the resulting data is a mix of both likes and dislikes. Help students pick a question that will split the remaining data cleanly into the two groups (multiple possible questions would work, but “Is the critics rating at least 8.5?” is one example).\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "215852f9-0322-4454-ac7f-8e6d9bb0c6d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df7d848b-4475-471a-86a5-9a43aeebd550",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students build a decision tree classifier model using Python and `scikit-learn`.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Implementing Decision Tree Learning"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df7d848b-4475-471a-86a5-9a43aeebd550",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d55174d9-012e-4810-bf67-0cbebf51b06c",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve already seen how we can use `scikit-learn` to train a classifier that uses nearest neighbor classification. We import `KNeighborsClassifier`, use it to create a model, and then fit the model to the training data.\n\nIf we want Python to use a decision tree to classify the data instead, we can just import `DecisionTreeClassifier` instead. We fit the model to the data and use the model in the exact same way as we did before.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d55174d9-012e-4810-bf67-0cbebf51b06c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d97b6e76-5812-419b-8229-19aa11a8d099",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Make Recommendations"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d97b6e76-5812-419b-8229-19aa11a8d099",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be75e295-13b9-46ae-afad-3f9374fef8e2",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be75e295-13b9-46ae-afad-3f9374fef8e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d40cd71-b427-4520-a023-92e93e8fd978",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students use `scikit-learn`’s `DecisionTreeClassifier` to construct a decision tree.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students about how their decision tree learned to classify.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through computing impurity, help them with common places where they might get stuck.\n- The first task for students is understanding the data available to them. Help students look at the CSV file provided to them and identify the features and labels. For students who aren’t sure, remind them that the label is what the model is trying to predict: in this case, the task is to predict whether a friend will like something or not, so the “liked” column is the label. All of the other columns are features: they are used as input to help make decisions.\n- If students get stuck about the syntax for building, training, and scoring a classifier, you can encourage them to ask AI Tutor. You can also remind students of the structure they used for training nearest neighbor classifiers: the code for building decision tree classifiers is very similar.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand what a decision tree is and how a computer comes up with one. Students should be able to identify questions that a decision tree asks and how those questions are used to help decide whether data belongs to one category or another. Students should also be able to identify the role that training data plays in helping the computer decide which questions will do the best job of splitting the data into categories. If students struggle here, it can be helpful to visualize the decision tree learning process: look at the visualizations generated by students’ code and talk with students about what the computer has learned and what process a computer would then follow to use the decision tree to make predictions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d40cd71-b427-4520-a023-92e93e8fd978",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17490b0b-3aba-4131-a7ff-ca0f3b70b789"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cf05fda-9048-4e09-a5f6-0014f0f58460",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cf05fda-9048-4e09-a5f6-0014f0f58460",
        "lesson_activity.key": "50a0d601-533d-4853-89ec-1934ece17b7c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "554f9d5f-7bde-4324-93f0-1bd6e8217703",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the context for today’s lesson."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "554f9d5f-7bde-4324-93f0-1bd6e8217703",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a18a477d-94c4-4762-96fc-83c009daefac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9f76c59-6c34-431a-a3e3-fd787fb3c002",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will be playing the role of game designers. Game designers often choose the numbers that shape a game before anyone plays it. Some examples include how fast a timer runs and how hard each level is. Game designers test the game and adjust those numbers until it plays right. Today, your game studio is making a game called Lantern Deep, a game where you climb down floors with a lantern that slowly burns out. Each floor you climb down is a different level.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9f76c59-6c34-431a-a3e3-fd787fb3c002",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a18a477d-94c4-4762-96fc-83c009daefac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d3e36b18-eba4-44d4-b42f-0f7f929b1d3b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s Career Journeys connection: game designers.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d3e36b18-eba4-44d4-b42f-0f7f929b1d3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a18a477d-94c4-4762-96fc-83c009daefac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1fe9a510-a71c-4d1e-8d8f-818566fe1d84",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Game designers are the people who make games feel the way they feel. They build the levels, they design the rules, and they choose the numbers that make the game so unique. Rarely do they get these numbers right the first time. They pick a number, they play, they watch other people play, and they adjust. Game studios also use tools to help with that work, including classifiers that sort playtest results into piles. Those tools have numbers to choose too.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1fe9a510-a71c-4d1e-8d8f-818566fe1d84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a18a477d-94c4-4762-96fc-83c009daefac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3358fe61-4b44-4e28-9ea2-f0e7feab883d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the question of the day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3358fe61-4b44-4e28-9ea2-f0e7feab883d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a18a477d-94c4-4762-96fc-83c009daefac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d488904e-d8ae-436d-abc3-25f0720a5cb4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students come to understand hyperparameters by finding the best value for one of the nearest-neighbors classifiers’ hyperparameters—the number of neighbors to consider when making a prediction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Tuning Report Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to read the first page of their Activity Guide and choose a classifier to tune.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The Tuning Report"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d488904e-d8ae-436d-abc3-25f0720a5cb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2ac51e74-e13c-431b-9d8a-592c8f7ec01b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The game studio uses three nearest-neighbors classifiers to make decisions about game development. You’ll choose one of these classifiers to tune today. Choose the classifier you want to tune, then turn to the page with that classifier.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2ac51e74-e13c-431b-9d8a-592c8f7ec01b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2aec0bdc-d8e5-4159-b57f-21a7af1bc81c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students about 7 minutes to identify the classifier’s predictions when accounting for different numbers of neighbors.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for these student challenges:\n- Some students might count the squares as neighbors. Only the labeled dots count; remind students that the squares are the new log entries that don’t have categories yet and shouldn’t be counted.\n- Students might not remember how to classify with 3 or 5 neighbors. Ask these students: “Of your three (or five) nearest neighbors, how many belong to each category?”\n- Students might have trouble determining which values are nearest. Ask students: “Which dot looks closest on the paper?” The grid is square by design to help with measurement.\n- Students might try to fill in the True Verdict early. Those come from the reveal; students should leave the column empty for now.\n- Students might struggle with the Quit Prediction classifier, which has some close calls to make when finding nearest neighbors. You can help students measure and identify the nearest neighbors, especially the third and fifth.\n- If students finish early, ask: “Before the reveal, which setting do you think scored best on your page, and why?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the true verdicts for each value students classified. Prompt students to copy the appropriate column into the True Verdict column on their classifier’s page."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2aec0bdc-d8e5-4159-b57f-21a7af1bc81c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca8b7b9f-d3e4-4a6c-8838-971f3256cc43",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You figured out how your classifier would classify each of these values when accounting for different numbers of neighbors. Here are the true labels your classifier should have predicted. Find the column with your classifier. Then, copy that column into the \"True Verdict\" column of your classifier’s table.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca8b7b9f-d3e4-4a6c-8838-971f3256cc43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13905a73-20ed-4079-84b7-7e191ca077ff",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to turn to the last page of their Activity Guide and assess how many of their classifiers predictions were correct at each number of neighbors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13905a73-20ed-4079-84b7-7e191ca077ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbdb2f02-7e8a-4a1b-9a71-73de959ade18",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, assess how well your classifier performed when accounting for each number of neighbors. The number of neighbors your classifier considers is a setting you can change. You want to find the value for that setting that results in the most accurate classifier.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbdb2f02-7e8a-4a1b-9a71-73de959ade18",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a78e8de-030e-4abf-9838-c215a5e942a7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which setting scored best on your page? Worst? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on the process of finding the best number of neighbors to consider. Strong responses will notice that accounting for only one nearest neighbor resulted in the worst classifier: by looking at only one neighbor, the classifier fails to find an overall pattern. In discussion, students may realize that one setting was not the best everywhere. In some cases, accounting for three or five neighbors scores the same. In other cases, accounting for five neighbors is best. Use this opportunity to indicate there’s often no one correct answer when tuning a classifier, and that the best choice may depend on the dataset one is working with.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *hyperparameter*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a78e8de-030e-4abf-9838-c215a5e942a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39de2603-1f02-46fb-b542-c8e6110f532a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The setting you just evaluated by hand has a name: a hyperparameter. A hyperparameter is a setting you choose before training that changes how a model works. In a nearest-neighbors classifier, the number of neighbors to consider is a hyperparameter.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider differentiating \"hyperparameter\" from \"parameter,\" a vocabulary term students learned in Unit 1."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39de2603-1f02-46fb-b542-c8e6110f532a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5e34199-98df-4d72-89aa-f4dc2cb7df0b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *tuning*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5e34199-98df-4d72-89aa-f4dc2cb7df0b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f03bf636-7fff-4fd7-8f25-478ebc061bde",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Often, AI engineers will test various values of a hyperparameter in order to choose the best one—just like you did! The intended result is a model that performs better overall.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f03bf636-7fff-4fd7-8f25-478ebc061bde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5310cffb-62ac-49e6-991c-a00b7d482f9b",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students tune a nearest-neighbors classifier, evaluating its accuracy as it considers various numbers of neighbors. Students pick the number of neighbors the evidence supports.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the program students will write.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Tune the Studio's Classifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5310cffb-62ac-49e6-991c-a00b7d482f9b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6fac6956-b419-47a3-8f65-912b970c769f",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here is the studio’s build for a classifier like the one you explored in the Activity Guide. It loads eighty logs from a file, splits them into training and test sets, and scales the features. Then, it trains a nearest-neighbors classifier—but not in the usual way.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6fac6956-b419-47a3-8f65-912b970c769f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fef396a1-5ee5-4fa4-9273-a5456d1060cb",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What will this loop print?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect the goal of this program to what they did in the Activity Guide. Strong responses will state that the loop prints the classifier’s accuracy as it considers each number of neighbors. By testing the classifier’s accuracy in this way, an engineer could know for what value of `n_neighbors` the classifier performs best.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example code. Point at line 3 and explain why a model is created inside of a loop: `n_neighbors` is set when the classifier is created, so a new `n_neighbor` setting needs a new classifier. Then, point at line 5: `model.score` provides the accuracy of the classifier. Finally, identify the hyperparameter explicitly: the `n_neighbors` parameter of `KNeighborsClassifier` is one of the model’s hyperparameters—the one that is being tested.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the random_state parameter of train_test_split, so students know how to control for random splits of training and test sets."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fef396a1-5ee5-4fa4-9273-a5456d1060cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b58df0b3-5073-4b85-909c-81f6cddc02b7",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you’re tuning your model’s hyperparameters, you’ll want to be sure its change in performance is due only to your tuning and not to random splits in your training and test sets. Passing an argument to the `random_state` parameter of `train_test_split` ensures you’ll get the same training and test set splits every time—so long as the argument is the same. The number can be anything, so long as it stays the same during your testing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b58df0b3-5073-4b85-909c-81f6cddc02b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff75a4f4-8518-48ee-8370-8b2fa186830b",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Tune the Studio's Classifiers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff75a4f4-8518-48ee-8370-8b2fa186830b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c875911-ffbb-4822-a2f0-d68a2f426fd4",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c875911-ffbb-4822-a2f0-d68a2f426fd4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29d6de73-5a0f-477f-98f6-e1d14494ae69",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students tune a classifier of their choice, finding the best value for `n_neighbors`.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, look out for:\n- A report where every row shows the same accuracy. The model was created once, above the loop, so every pass trains the same setting. Ask: “Where does the classifier find out what setting to use?”\n- A loop that trains but never scores, or scores the training logs instead of the test logs. The report must use `scaled_test_inputs` and `test_outputs`, the same twenty logs every time.\n- Students unsure which setting their report supports. Ask: “Which row has the highest accuracy? That is the setting the evidence supports.” For the Level Difficulty classifier, the best value is 9. On the Speedrun Screening and Quit Prediction classifiers, the best value is 7.\n- Students surprised that the code’s winner differs from their paper winner. That is expected: five logs is a small playtest, and eighty is a better one. Have them compare the shape, not the numbers: low at 1, a peak in the middle, a fall by 15.\n- Students done early. Ask: “What happens at 25 or 45? Add it to the list and find out.” (Anything above 60 will raise an error, since there are only 60 training logs; that is a fine thing to discover too.)",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 is the Evaluating and Improving Models (Stage 3) assessment point. As you circulate, note who writes the loop independently and can say (a) why the day-one setting scores below the peak and (b) why the far settings fall off (Level 3 on the rubric), versus who needs prompting to create the model inside the loop or to read the peak off the report."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29d6de73-5a0f-477f-98f6-e1d14494ae69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1eebba8-fefa-4489-a964-80e2255ae741",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Debrief what students have discovered by tuning their classifier.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does accuracy start to decrease after a certain number of neighbors?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to consider why more neighbors isn’t always better. Strong responses will suggest that, as the classifier looks at more neighbors, it starts to become underfit: that is, it starts to lose the real pattern. If students struggle to identify the cause, prompt them to visualize what it would look like to consider, say, all neighbors. When considering all of the neighbors, the pattern the classifier is supposed to identify is completely lost.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview hyperparameters of other models, which students have the option to explore in more depth in the upcoming project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1eebba8-fefa-4489-a964-80e2255ae741",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d62958dc-9de4-4904-a43b-1703c562f208",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Nearest neighbors isn’t the only model with hyperparameters.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d62958dc-9de4-4904-a43b-1703c562f208",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cbe2d70-5b61-4b82-895e-ca4081973034",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce maximum depth as a hyperparameter for `DecisionTreeClassifier`."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cbe2d70-5b61-4b82-895e-ca4081973034",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e9c00b4-8c37-44e3-a857-b0488e21d720",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of a decision tree’s hyperparameters is its maximum depth—that is, the maximum number of questions in the tree. Decision trees can often overfit: they may use questions that perform well on the training set, but that aren’t relevant to the test set. By tweaking a tree’s maximum depth, you can prevent overfitting by limiting the number of questions in the tree.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e9c00b4-8c37-44e3-a857-b0488e21d720",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5738cc61-3edd-4e3f-bac0-94916b9cf2f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfaaf53c-6221-428c-b053-5c3584730392",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. A strong answer: the decisions are the model’s hyperparameters, the numbers you choose before training, like `n_neighbors`, and you make them by tuning, trying settings and keeping the one that scores best.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfaaf53c-6221-428c-b053-5c3584730392",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f9100f60-5e26-4749-9d2d-a86e02a3b3f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d66fd263-3b2e-412f-8c63-309f941ad731",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Classification Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d66fd263-3b2e-412f-8c63-309f941ad731",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77f38cc4-5b21-42eb-94ea-3ab47a996170",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in picking a problem, and accompanying dataset, for their classification project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Picking a Problem"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77f38cc4-5b21-42eb-94ea-3ab47a996170",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09bb7b5b-02d6-4cbc-9bb4-3869814ed620",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this project, you’ll pick a classification problem to solve. To do so, you’ll build either a nearest-neighbors classifier or a decision tree classifier. Today, you’ll pick a problem to solve and learn one way to reason about whether nearest neighbors or a decision tree is the better approach to solving that problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09bb7b5b-02d6-4cbc-9bb4-3869814ed620",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bb73572-3081-4062-aca6-8beeaed8f099",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete Step 1 of their Project Planning Guide: Picking a Problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Students can choose from among four different problems: Fishing for Bass, Knitting to Completion, Selling Secondhand, and Healing After Surgery. While students frame these problems as classification problems in their Project Planning Guide, check to make sure students are correctly identifying the features and labels for each problem.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "A label is what a machine learning model seeks to predict. A feature is something the model uses to make that prediction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bb73572-3081-4062-aca6-8beeaed8f099",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "722012dc-aa1b-42ef-9a33-5d0da3b00202",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Give students a framework for choosing between a nearest-neighbors classifier and a decision tree classifier to solve their classification problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to spend 3–5 minutes completing Step 2 of their Project Planning Guide: Adopting an Approach.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Adopting an Approach"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "722012dc-aa1b-42ef-9a33-5d0da3b00202",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "494074f8-500d-4384-9be5-8f25a261b01c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Remember that, in this project, you’ll decide the best classifier to build for your chosen problem. Let’s look at one way you can decide which approach might be better.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "494074f8-500d-4384-9be5-8f25a261b01c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd79931e-bbd5-4e01-a454-95d89ca9a37c",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review and discuss students’ hypotheses from the Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd79931e-bbd5-4e01-a454-95d89ca9a37c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de259049-e68a-4291-92b3-867ee8890ef7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s one of the datasets you saw in your Project Planning Guide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de259049-e68a-4291-92b3-867ee8890ef7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d3d6713-349d-4300-9c2d-f0392625ed7f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you draw the decision boundary for this dataset? Which approach did you think would be best for this problem?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students’ attempts at drawing a decision boundary, as well as their intuitions about the best approach. Strong responses will indicate a good decision boundary could be created from two lines—one vertical and one horizontal. Each line represents a cutoff along a particular feature. Decisions would then be made based on whether a single feature is above or below its cutoff—mirroring how a decision tree classifies data. The shape of this problem, then, suggests a decision tree. Students who say nearest neighbors would be a good approach to this problem are not incorrect: the two pure clusters of data do suggest nearest neighbors could work well too. The point of this example, however, is to illustrate the kind of problem to which a decision tree is particularly suited: a problem where one can successfully classify data by making \"cuts\" along individual features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the example decision boundary partway through the discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Summarize the takeaway from the prior discussion. Describe how classification problems with decision boundaries that are vertical or horizontal lend themselves well to an approach that uses a decision tree."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d3d6713-349d-4300-9c2d-f0392625ed7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "505163d9-7bb7-4d1b-bc29-3af156dae9f4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A decision tree relies on splitting features. Its decision boundaries are vertical or horizontal.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "505163d9-7bb7-4d1b-bc29-3af156dae9f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e041bbb-4078-438d-8bec-abc5e331529f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Continue reviewing and discussing students’ hypotheses from the Project Planning Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e041bbb-4078-438d-8bec-abc5e331529f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c44729d-397d-4a49-9eb4-ce734073567e",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s another of the datasets you saw in your Project Planning Guide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c44729d-397d-4a49-9eb4-ce734073567e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0c799f9-48b3-4e7b-bb7d-8c6a02c316ce",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you draw the decision boundary for this dataset? Which approach did you think would be best for this problem?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Again surface students’ attempts at drawing a decision boundary, as well as their intuitions about the best approach. Strong responses will indicate a good decision boundary could be created with a diagonal line. On one side of this decision boundary, data is of one class. On the other side, data is of another class. A diagonal decision boundary, though, is difficult for a decision tree to approximate: as students have learned, a decision tree relies on splitting features and so can only make vertical or horizontal “cuts.” Nearest neighbors, by contrast, doesn’t rely on splitting features: it relies on clusters of examples. The shape of this problem, then, suggests nearest neighbors. Students who suggest a decision tree could solve this problem are not incorrect: a decision tree could solve this problem, but it would likely be large—it would have many questions and branches. The point of this example is to illustrate the kind of problem to which nearest neighbors is particularly suited: a problem with clear clusters of examples that can’t be divided neatly by splitting along individual features.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reveal the example decision boundary partway through the discussion.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Summarize the takeaway from the prior discussion. Describe how classification problems with clear clusters of examples, regardless of the shape of the boundaries between them, lend themselves well to an approach that uses nearest neighbors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0c799f9-48b3-4e7b-bb7d-8c6a02c316ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bac4dc48-2830-425f-8477-94db1d09a2f3",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Nearest neighbors relies on clusters of examples. Its decision boundaries can be any shape.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bac4dc48-2830-425f-8477-94db1d09a2f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b79c029-7c42-4824-ac61-fe13011efede",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review key takeaways from a comparison of decision trees with nearest neighbors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b79c029-7c42-4824-ac61-fe13011efede",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a7ad7ba-8b41-4691-a99c-9376325ab31d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen, a decision tree relies on being able to split data along individual features. This means it doesn’t really care if two features are on different scales, and that it ultimately creates general, interpretable rules. Meanwhile, nearest neighbors relies on clusters of labeled data points. That means it works well when features are of a similar scale, and it’s able to find decision boundaries of any shape.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a7ad7ba-8b41-4691-a99c-9376325ab31d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a39e7e9c-e767-466b-9583-8025968e284e",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classification Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a39e7e9c-e767-466b-9583-8025968e284e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d028647-dd32-469c-8fca-778ea6f6a474",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students see a visualization of the dataset they’ve chosen and consider the shape of its decision boundaries. They reflect in their Project Planning Guide.\n- **Level 2:** Students chat with AI to identify their approach to solving their classification problem. They reflect in their Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Levels 1–2, look for:\n- **Level 1:** Students who could use additional help interpreting the plot of data shown to them. Each problem’s dataset provides students with three features: two numerical and one categorical. To visualize these three features on a 2D plot, each plot displays multiple figures: one for each value of the categorical feature. Each figure then plots the two numerical features, labeling each point with the label a classifier should predict. Guide a student in interpreting the visualization one figure at a time. Suggest they think of each figure as a subset of the total dataset where their categorical feature takes a particular category.\n- **Level 2:** Students who could use additional support describing their problem to AI. The better a student can describe their problem, the better AI will be at suggesting an appropriate approach. Look for students to be able to describe the shape of the decision boundary based on the visualizations in Level 1, the types of features their problem’s dataset provides to them, and whether those features have similar or dissimilar scales."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d028647-dd32-469c-8fca-778ea6f6a474",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a510162-cf98-4af4-ae49-2b8d2c1d3367",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide. They synthesize what they’ve learned in this lesson to choose the kind of classifier they will build to solve their problem. Remind students there’s no single right choice—either option is okay if students can explain their reasoning for it.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a510162-cf98-4af4-ae49-2b8d2c1d3367",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c405853-ee92-4ef2-9227-10030016c99d",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we return, you’ll implement the classifier you’ve chosen.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c405853-ee92-4ef2-9227-10030016c99d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "337673f4-57a6-4e81-a4fb-f043d9e6e421"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf871ade-7c68-4329-b798-66b43ec4f389",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to discuss in groups of 2–3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What problem are you working on? What kind of classifier have you decided to build?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Survey students’ decisions from Day 1 and allow students to check their reasoning against another classmate’s reasoning. Strong responses will make a connection between the dataset a given problem provides and the choice of classifier, relying on the principles introduced in Day 1. Conclude the discussion by saying that it’s okay for a student to change the classifier they’ve decided to build if they’ve been persuaded to change their approach.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally structure this discussion as a whole-class discussion. Doing so offers fewer opportunities for all students to speak, but potentially allows all students to hear how others are approaching the problem that they, too, are solving."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf871ade-7c68-4329-b798-66b43ec4f389",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ada97722-5295-44de-8bd6-a7accbe1ba41",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students through implementing and visualizing their classifier of choice.",
        "name": "Classification Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ada97722-5295-44de-8bd6-a7accbe1ba41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2baa99e-060f-49ad-bf4d-874e49da8fef",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you’ll focus on implementing the model you’ve chosen. You’ll encode your inputs and outputs, split and scale your data, fit your model to that data, and finally visualize what your model has learned. Much of your work today will be guided by the levels and your Project Planning Guide. Complete as much of Levels 3–5 as you can, and wait until we reconvene as a class before continuing to Levels 6 and beyond.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2baa99e-060f-49ad-bf4d-874e49da8fef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14ac92e3-6325-4501-bbe5-544d874221af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classification Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14ac92e3-6325-4501-bbe5-544d874221af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fcc18a8f-9b47-49cb-9abb-8f98cd71a0a2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 3–5.\n- **Level 3:** Students encode their inputs and outputs. In their Project Planning Guide, they describe how they’ve chosen to encode their features.\n- **Level 4:** Students split and scale their data, then fit their model to that data. In their Project Planning Guide, they justify their decision to scale—or not to scale—their data.\n- **Level 5:** Students call a helper function to visualize their model. In their Project Planning Guide, they reflect on this visualization to understand what their model has learned.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- **Level 3:** Students who need additional support to encode their features. Students may forget to cast their numeric data to an int or a float, or forget how to use OneHotEncoder (if they decide to use it). Remind students about steps that are easily overlooked, and prompt students to ask AI Tutor for assistance if they need a reminder about how to use a particular scikit-learn class—in this level or future ones.\n- **Level 4:** Students who aren’t sure whether they need to scale their data. Encourage students to first think critically about their model choice. Because a decision tree splits on individual features, and doesn’t compare values across features, scale isn’t particularly relevant. But because nearest neighbors weights feature values equally in its distance calculations, scale is very relevant. Even if a student is using nearest neighbors with two numerical features roughly equal in magnitude, their encoding of categorical data—using e.g., 1’s and 0’s—will almost always mean their feature encodings have different orders of magnitude that should adopt the same scale.\n- **Level 5:** Students who could use additional guidance on how to interpret the visualization of their model. If a student is making a decision tree, they will see a text-based representation in their console. If a student is making a nearest-neighbors classifier, they will see a plot that shades the areas in which the model will make a given decision. Encourage decision tree students to read their visualization from left to right, top to bottom, noting where each path leads. Encourage nearest-neighbors students to look at the shaded regions and imagine if the points in that region do, in fact, belong to a given class. You may also consider using upcoming slides to introduce the visualizations by example.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Today’s class time is primarily guided by the levels and the Project Planning Guide. If some students finish the lesson’s work more quickly than anticipated, consider pairing them with students who are working on the same problem. Options for structuring this time include, but aren’t limited to:\n- Pair programming, in which the student who’s finished early can make suggestions—but can’t write code for another student.\n- Pair discussion, in which students who are working on the same problem collaboratively discuss their approach.\n\nThe best structure to choose depends on your judgment."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fcc18a8f-9b47-49cb-9abb-8f98cd71a0a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "efb8bc08-feea-44ab-9d93-545bb7a77150",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students visualize their models, consider explaining how to read each visualization. First, introduce the visualization for a decision tree."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "efb8bc08-feea-44ab-9d93-545bb7a77150",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ef0e078-6936-4051-a9ca-a0908b3b27e2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s a visualization of a decision tree. We can read this tree from top to bottom, left to right. It describes the questions a tree will ask, and how each leads to another. Notice that, when we reach a \"leaf\" of the tree—a point at which there are no more questions—we see how many elements from each class are in the final group.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have additional time, invite students to consider whether this model appears to be over, under, or appropriately fit. Offer that, in this context, an overfit model would be one that asks too many questions: it would capture the specific pattern in the training data, but not the general pattern. An underfit model would be one that asks too few questions: it wouldn’t capture any patterns at all. This model appears to be appropriately fit. It asks a few questions that seem to capture a general pattern and, for the most part, it categorizes examples correctly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ef0e078-6936-4051-a9ca-a0908b3b27e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "738ac006-0fb2-4ab4-a762-c35033be544f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the visualization of a nearest-neighbors classifier."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "738ac006-0fb2-4ab4-a762-c35033be544f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "991a6644-2898-4b5c-882d-49e341b9f678",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this visualization, we see two shaded regions. Each region represents the area in which the classifier would consider a new data point to belong to the given class. We see two figures because of our categorical variable—whether or not there exists a deadline. The first figure represents examples where there is not a deadline. The second figure represents examples where there is a deadline.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have additional time, invite students to consider whether this model appears to be over, under, or appropriately fit. Offer that, in this context, an overfit model would be one with very jagged decision regions: it would capture the specific pattern in the training data, but not the general pattern. An underfit model would be one with decision regions that don’t align with the data at all: it wouldn’t capture any pattern. This model appears to be appropriately fit, if not slightly overfit. The decision regions do seem to capture a general pattern, but they do have jagged edges that might reflect noise rather than anything genuine. This model uses a value of 3 for `n_neighbors`."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "991a6644-2898-4b5c-882d-49e341b9f678",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "371e9edf-577a-40d3-85b2-1a33fe5d9865",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "371e9edf-577a-40d3-85b2-1a33fe5d9865",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9367135b-6bd2-4ff5-8e99-7f297f42025e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72cd0724-9198-4b21-aade-72ea82af27d4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to find a partner, then share and explain their model’s visualization.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, optionally call on students to share what they discussed with their partner."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72cd0724-9198-4b21-aade-72ea82af27d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86b4d3c6-a336-4026-a270-94595d42e35b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Review key concepts students will use to complete their project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review classification metrics.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Classification Metrics and Hyperparameters Review"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86b4d3c6-a336-4026-a270-94595d42e35b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8524249-5f2e-49b5-b6c4-bd39501bf19b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today you’ll focus on evaluating and refining your classifier. Let’s review the key classification metrics first.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8524249-5f2e-49b5-b6c4-bd39501bf19b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "518f8868-48d1-434b-a6ed-ab209bc6eda3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to recall the definitions of accuracy, recall, and precision. For each metric, invite students to share what they remember about the definition. Encourage students to use examples. Then, reveal the definition of the metric. Continue until you’ve reviewed all three metrics."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "518f8868-48d1-434b-a6ed-ab209bc6eda3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "374b5d3d-18c8-4135-aec1-8976432ea02a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen before, your classifier’s context can determine which metrics you care the most about. Let’s look at how you can calculate each of these metrics in `scikit-learn`.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This slide could be introduced as a call and response activity if you or your students prefer."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "374b5d3d-18c8-4135-aec1-8976432ea02a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b76e6da-781e-4088-8b4c-e998f7b380cf",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce how to find scores for accuracy, precision, and recall in `scikit-learn`."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b76e6da-781e-4088-8b4c-e998f7b380cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "573076c1-9409-4f5a-80b9-58479040521e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In addition to the scoring functions you’ve seen, `scikit-learn` comes with three functions to calculate accuracy, recall, and precision. Each requires two arguments—the outputs you expect, and the outputs your model has predicted. The recall and precision functions also need a keyword argument, `pos_label`. This is the name of the label for which you are calculating recall and precision.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For reference, `pos_label` stands for \"positive label.\" `accuracy_score` is performing the same calculation as `model.score`."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "573076c1-9409-4f5a-80b9-58479040521e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "928e298d-1be9-4512-8ae9-c6549d92b165",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review hyperparameters in decision trees and nearest neighbors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "928e298d-1be9-4512-8ae9-c6549d92b165",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e6796d7-b4cd-47a4-a8fb-122ac18c0c2a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once you’ve decided on the metrics you’ll prioritize, you’ll tweak your model’s hyperparameters to see if you can boost your model’s performance.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e6796d7-b4cd-47a4-a8fb-122ac18c0c2a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85da2660-112c-4747-86c8-b8dd474dc8fa",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review maximum depth as a hyperparameter for `DecisionTreeClassifier`."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85da2660-112c-4747-86c8-b8dd474dc8fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b155bc90-ca01-46ef-96ab-1cce9eb0a436",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of a decision tree’s hyperparameters is its maximum depth—that is, the maximum number of questions in the tree. Decision trees can often overfit: they may use questions that perform well on the training set, but that aren’t relevant to the test set. By tweaking a tree’s maximum depth, you can prevent overfitting by limiting the number of questions in the tree.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b155bc90-ca01-46ef-96ab-1cce9eb0a436",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b32ddfa1-003c-41ef-9e43-4733093fc0e7",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the number of neighbors, or k, as a hyperparameter of nearest neighbors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b32ddfa1-003c-41ef-9e43-4733093fc0e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f4c70d0-07bf-461b-86f1-884d643c29d2",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the hyperparameters of a nearest-neighbors classifier is the number of neighbors it considers. As you’ve seen in prior lessons, there’s usually an optimal number of neighbors to consider. Too few, and the model may become overfit. Too many, and the model may become underfit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f4c70d0-07bf-461b-86f1-884d643c29d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbc00f1e-00e2-4c0a-b8ab-b00fb7c8809c",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the `random_state` parameter of `train_test_split`, so students know how to control for random splits of training and test sets."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbc00f1e-00e2-4c0a-b8ab-b00fb7c8809c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64e085bd-869d-4bcd-9ef9-f614be9f7dad",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When you’re tuning your model’s hyperparameters, you’ll want to be sure its change in performance is due only to your tuning and not to random splits in your training and test sets. Passing an argument to the `random_state` parameter of `train_test_split` ensures you’ll get the same training and test set splits every time—so long as the argument is the same. The number can be anything, so long as it stays the same during your testing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64e085bd-869d-4bcd-9ef9-f614be9f7dad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2677a245-a18f-4128-83a3-7606acc41f30",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in finishing their project by evaluating their classifier and tuning its hyperparameters.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Classification Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2677a245-a18f-4128-83a3-7606acc41f30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e58f46d5-fbf9-4e17-ba26-a9d3f63d7151",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s time to use what you’ve learned and reviewed to evaluate and tune your classifier.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e58f46d5-fbf9-4e17-ba26-a9d3f63d7151",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f56bf66e-8dee-45e3-814c-91cce2eb6fa1",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Classification Project"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f56bf66e-8dee-45e3-814c-91cce2eb6fa1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7258a534-fcc4-4406-b844-f5c1d54bdebd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "711992a5-793e-404c-b217-d4b399d18e0b",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 6–7.\n- **Level 6:** Students use their model to make predictions from their test inputs, evaluate those predictions using metrics of their choice, and adjust their model’s hyperparameters to optimize for those metrics. They justify their choices of metrics in their Project Planning Guide.\n- **Level 7:** Students answer a reflection question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for:\n- Students who aren’t sure how to get the information they need to calculate their model’s accuracy, recall, and precision. Remind students they can call `model.predict` with their (scaled) test inputs to get a list of predictions. They can then compare these predictions with their test outputs, calling the `scikit-learn` functions they just learned about. Encourage students to print the results of the scoring functions.\n- Students who get different results on their metrics each time they run their program. Ensure a student is using the `random_state` parameter of `train_test_split`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Debrief students’ reflection questions and the project as a whole.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How successful was your model at solving your problem? What would you test, change, or approach differently if you had more time?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on the results from, and their experience of, building their classifier. Strong responses will use a student’s chosen metrics to justify their perception of how successfully their classifier performed. Students’ thoughts about what they would test, change, or approach differently can vary widely. Responses could include trying another type of classifier, further tweaking hyperparameters, or even choosing a different problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Celebrate! Acknowledge students have now independently solved a problem with machine learning, using the knowledge they’ve gained throughout this unit and the previous."
      },
